{
  "module": "The transformer block",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "feed-forward",
    "title": "The feed-forward sublayer",
    "summary": "The small two-layer network applied to every token's vector on its own: its formula, its shapes, the wider middle, and why the same weights are used at every position.",
    "covers": ["ffn"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "layers-hidden-states",
        "reason": "It transforms each token's hidden-state vector."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "sequence-shapes",
        "reason": "It is described by its shapes, d_model × d_ff and d_ff × d_model."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "per-token-network",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Each layer contains a "
            },
            {
              "text": "feed-forward",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " sublayer, often written "
            },
            {
              "text": "FFN",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". It is a small network that takes "
            },
            {
              "text": "one token's vector",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " and transforms it, without looking at any other token."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It has two weight matrices with a nonlinear step between them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 58)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "the-formula",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "For a token's vector x (a row of length d_model):"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "\\mathrm{FFN}(x) = \\mathrm{act}(x W_1 + b_1)\\, W_2 + b_2",
          "spoken": "FFN of x equals the activation of x times W 1 plus b 1, times W 2, plus b 2"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "W₁",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " has shape d_model × d_ff"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "W₂",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " has shape d_ff × d_model"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "b₁",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " and "
              },
              {
                "text": "b₂",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " are bias vectors, and "
              },
              {
                "text": "act",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " is an activation function"
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The deck writes the same thing with the matrices on the left of x. Here the vector is a row, as in the earlier lessons."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 58)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "wider-middle",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The middle width "
            },
            {
              "text": "d_ff",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " is usually bigger than d_model. The classic choice is "
            },
            {
              "text": "d_ff = 4 · d_model",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": 2,048 when d_model = 512."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "So the vector is "
            },
            {
              "text": "widened",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " from d_model to d_ff by W₁, and "
            },
            {
              "text": "narrowed",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " back to d_model by W₂. The output has the same length as the input."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 58)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "the-activation",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Between the two matrices is an "
            },
            {
              "text": "activation",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " function. A common simple one is ReLU: ReLU(z) = max(0, z), which keeps positive numbers and turns negative ones into 0."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It matters. Two matrix multiplications with nothing between them are the same as one bigger matrix multiplication. The activation is what makes the FFN a "
            },
            {
              "text": "nonlinear",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " transformation."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 58, 59)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "toy-ffn",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A toy case: d_model = 2, d_ff = 3, ReLU, biases left out. W₁ = (1, 0, −1 / 0, 1, 1) and W₂ = (1, 2 / 0, 1 / 3, 0)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "x = (1, −1). Then x W₁ = (1, -1, -2)"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "After ReLU: (1, 0, 0)"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Times W₂: (1, 2)"
              }
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "With x = (0, 2): x W₁ = (0, 2, 2), after ReLU (0, 2, 2), and the output is (6, 2)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 58)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "every-position",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The FFN is applied to "
            },
            {
              "text": "each position independently, with the same weights at every position",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". The vector at position 3 goes through exactly the same W₁ and W₂ as the vector at position 8."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "So two positions holding the same vector get the same FFN output, and what happens at one position does not depend on the other positions."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 58, 59)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
